TI  - Genetic, neural, and environmental factors related to the development of callous-unemotional and disruptive behaviors across development
AU  - Perlstein, Samantha C.
PY  - 2025 2026
JO  - Dissertation Abstracts International: Section B: The Sciences and Engineering
SP  - 296
DO  - Ph.D.
AB  - Childhood disruptive behaviors predict risk for lifetime antisocial behavior. However, treatment responsiveness can be variable. This is attributed to a high degree of heterogeneity in etiology and presentation. To aid in the development of preventative and precision treatments knowledge is needed about shared and unique risk pathways across disruptive behavior symptom dimensions. The studies presented characterize genetic, neural, and environmental risk pathways to childhood disruptive behaviors and its dimensions, including callous-unemotional (CU) traits. Study One leverages neuroimaging to investigate shared versus unique neural correlates of externalizing psychopathology. Examining data from 11,878 preadolescents from the Adolescent Brain and Cognitive Development (ABCD) Study, I found amygdala activation to fearful faces was associated with higher general externalizing psychopathology. I found specific associations between CU traits and reduced dlPFC activation to fearful faces and ADHD symptoms and increased NAc activation to happy faces. Study Two draws from 772 twin pairs from the ABCD Study and examines whether parenting factors moderate genetic and environmental influences on CU traits, with and without covarying for comorbidity with conduct problems. Gene by environment interaction models demonstrated that environmental influences on CU traits were stronger among children reporting lower levels of parental acceptance and whose parents reported higher levels of psychopathology. Without covarying for conduct problems, environmental influences on CU traits were stronger among children reporting greater family conflict. Study Three utilizes data from 238 parent-child dyads followed from birth to age 2 to explore early risk factors for social communication difficulties within an ecological systems framework. I found that lower household income and mother-child postpartum bonding prospectively related to more social communication difficulties at age 2. Neighborhood resources were related to more social communication difficulties at age 1 and 2 at low and mean levels of mother-infant bonding, as well as at age 2 at low levels of household income. Together, this work demonstrates that individual (e.g., neural activation), family (e.g., acceptance, bonding), and neighborhood (e.g., resources) level characteristics can signal risk for broad and specific facets of disruptive behavior. These findings underscore the need for future research aimed at improving preventative and precision interventions. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
