AB  - Determined differences in incidence of developmental abnormalities among hospitalized psychiatric patients aged 16-30, with different EEG patterns, and differences in behavior, affect, and cognition between Ss with and without EEG dysrythmia. Ss were 16 patients with the 14 and 6 cps positive spike pattern, 14 with normal EEG, and 8 with right posterior slowing. Ss were from stable sociofamilial backgrounds and similar socioeconomic status. Results show significant differences between groups in developmental, affective, and cognitive functions. No differences between groups were found for neonatal complications, speech, and motor development, and social isolation in childhood. The most striking difference between groups was found in the incidence of significant illness in childhood. Results indicate that there are significant differences between groups of young hospitalized psychiatric patients on the basis of cerebral dysfunction, using EEG criteria. Findings indicate that varieties of psychopathology and cognitive and affective styles, are related to cerebral function and dysfunction. It is suggested that appropriate treatment programs take these factors into account. (16 ref.) (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
