AB  - Early adversity has profound long-term consequences for child development across domains. The effects of early adversity on structural and functional brain development were shown for infants under 12 months of life. However, the causal mechanisms of these effects remain relatively unexplored. Using a visual habituation task we investigated whether chaotic home environment may affect processing speed in 5.5 month-old infants (n = 71). We found detrimental effects of chaos on processing speed for complex but not for simple visual stimuli. No effects of socioeconomic status on infant processing speed were found although the sample was predominantly middle class.
