TY  - label.ris.referenceType.BOOK_CHAPTER
TI  - Neuroscientific approaches to optimize self-regulatory skills in children from backgrounds of poverty in different cultural contexts
AU  - Pakulak, E.
AU  - Lipina, S.J.
PY  - 2021
JO  - Quality Improvement in Early Childhood Education: International Perspectives on Enhancing Learning Outcomes
SP  - 199
EP  - 228
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-030-73182-3_11
AB  - It is increasingly recognized that early experiences associated with poverty are associated with changes in brain structure and function, in particular for regulatory systems that are both sensitive to chronic stress and important for school readiness and academic achievement (e.g., Lipina & Posner, Frontiers in Human Neuroscience, 6, 238, 2012; Pakulak, Stevens, & Neville, Annual Review of Psychology, 69, 2018). In addition, recent interdisciplinary models of intergenerational poverty emphasize the importance of considering broader social and cultural contexts, including the quality of early childhood education, in reaching a more comprehensive understanding of how different aspects of the environment contribute to chronic stress and differences in self-regulation (e.g., Blair & Raver, Annual Review of Psychology, 66, 711-731, 2015; McEwen & McEwen, Annual Review of Sociology, 43, 445-472, 2017). In this chapter, we review two parallel lines of research on these questions in two different cultural contexts, Argentina and the US. Both lines of research employ cognitive and neuroscientific methods to address both basic and applied research questions in educational contexts. Between 2002 and 2018, in the Unit of Applied Neurobiology we designed, implemented, and evaluated seven interventions aimed at optimizing the cognitive performance of children between four and five years of age from different districts of Argentina and from backgrounds of poverty (Goldin et al., Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 111(17), 6443-6448, 2014; Hermida et al., Trends in Neuroscience and Education, 4(1-2), 15-25, 2015; Pietto et al., Trends in Neuroscience and Education, 13, 34-42, 2018; Segretin et al., Frontiers in Psychology, 5, 205, 2014). All interventions were implemented in schools or childcare centers, using different strategies: exercise and cognitive training with toys or computer games, curricular adaptations, and play activities with mothers and children. Results revealed near and far transfer effects, depending on the type of approach, sample, and measurement (i.e., behavioral, electroencephalography). In the US, we developed, implemented, and assessed a two-generation intervention for preschoolers and their parents. The intervention targets attention, family stress, and self-regulation, and was found to reduce parenting stress and improve child behavior, cognition, and brain function for attention in families living in poverty (Neville et al., Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 2013). We subsequently developed and implemented a scaled-up delivery model of the intervention that was integrated into multiple preschool sites (Pakulak et al., New Directions for Child and Adolescent Development, 2017(158), 81-92, 2017); evaluation of the efficacy of this model is underway. The chapter will conclude with a critical discussion of the potential, as well as the limitations, of such approaches to ameliorate the effects of early adversity in different cultural contexts. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2021.
