TI  - Effects of infectious diseases on child and brain development and their association with poverty
AU  - Gale, S.D.
AU  - Hedges, D.W.
PY  - 2024
JO  - Infectious Diseases in Neurocognitive and Neuropsychiatric Medicine
SP  - 443
EP  - 460
DO  - 10.1093/oso/9780192870414.003.0025
AB  - Infectious diseases affect a large number of people including children worldwide, and infections during gestation and childhood can adversely affect brain development, resulting in cognitive and neuropsychiatric deficits. Numerous factors including climate change can increase the risk of maternal and childhood infection particularly in conditions of poverty and in low- and middle-income regions. Maternal and early childhood infections from cytomegalovirus, rubella, Zika virus, Epstein-Barr virus, Lyme disease, malaria, Toxoplasma gondii, toxocariasis, and maternal genital infections can affect fetal and child brain development and result in cognitive and neuropsychiatric dysfunction. Maternal gestational and childhood infections likely contribute to the overall burden of neuropsychiatric disease and cognitive dysfunction, suggesting fetal and early childhood origins for some neuropsychiatric and cognitive disorders. Treatment and prevention of maternal infectious diseases provide potentially effective methods of reducing the overall burden of neuropsychiatric disease and cognitive dysfunction. © Oxford University Press 2024. All rights reserved.
