TI  - Preverbal Development and Speech Perception
AU  - Panneton, R.
AU  - Bruce, M.
AU  - McFayden, T.
AU  - Taylor, C.
PY  - 2020
JO  - Encyclopedia of Infant and Early Childhood Development
SP  - 621
EP  - 629
DO  - 10.1016/B978-0-12-809324-5.21833-6
AB  - During infancy, natural and frequent interactions with caregivers provide young humans with opportunities to discover aspects of their worlds, including (but not limited to) information about themselves, about others, about objects, and about relations between infants, others, and/or objects. Much of this discovery happens in the context of communication - learning a language system within which this information emerges. This article focuses on general characteristics of linguistic awareness, before infants become fully productive users of language. Culling research from behavioral and neurophysiological studies, we discuss prenatal exposure to maternal sounds, early biases toward faces and voices, emerging awareness of native language structure, and the integration of language into co-occurring systems (e.g., emotion expression and regulation) during this exciting period of development. © 2020 Elsevier Inc.
