TI  - Tablet use affects preschoolers’ executive function: fNIRS evidence from the dimensional change card sort task
AU  - Li, H.
AU  - Wu, D.
AU  - Yang, J.
AU  - Luo, J.
AU  - Xie, S.
AU  - Chang, C.
PY  - 2021
JO  - Brain Sciences
VL  - 11
IS  - 5
DO  - 10.3390/brainsci11050567
AB  - This study aims to examine the impact of heavy use of tablets on preschoolers’ executive function during the Dimensional Change Card Sort (DCCS) task using the functional near-infrared spectroscopy (fNIRS). Altogether, 38 Chinese preschoolers (Mage = 5.0 years, SD = 0.69 years, 17 girls) completed the tasks before the COVID-19 lockdown. Eight children never used tablets, while 16 children were diagnosed as the ‘heavy-user’. The results indicated that: (1) the ŉon-user’ outperformed the ‘heavy-user’ with a significantly higher correct rate in the DCCS task; (2) the two groups differed significantly in the activation of the prefrontal cortex (BA 9): the ŉon-user’ pattern is normal and healthy, whereas the ‘heavy-user’ pattern is not normal and needs further exploration. © 2021 by the authors.
