TY  - JOUR
TI  - Early under-nutrition and mental performance
AU  - Van Heerden, J.R.
PY  - 1984
JO  - International Journal of Early Childhood
VL  - 16
IS  - 1
SP  - 10
EP  - 16
DO  - 10.1007/BF03174954
AB  - This paper reviews the effect of malnutrition during pregnancy on brain development and subsequent mental performance of the unborn child. The work discussed includes research done by Dr Stoch at the Red Cross Hospital in Cape Town. She concluded that her findings establish that early severe under-nutrition resulted in permanent reduction in brain size and impaired cognitive thinking. The considerable incidence of malnutrition both worldwide and in South Africa indicates that a large number of children must be at risk during the critical growth period of the brain which extends over the first two years of life. Marasmus and Kwashiorkor are described and the implications for Local Authorities and private organisations of continuing costly feeding schemes are considered. The relationship between the 'self perpetuating culture of poverty', malnutrition and illegitimacy is important and South Africa is by no means immune from this. A course of action which the South African Association for Early Childhood Education could take to publicise the relevant facts regarding severe malnutrition and its effect on mental achievement, is suggested. © 1984 Springer.
