TI  - Developmental follow-up of low-birth-weight infants
AU  - Vohr, B.R.
AU  - Hack, M.
PY  - 1982
JO  - Pediatric Clinics of North America
VL  - 29
IS  - 6
SP  - 1441
EP  - 1454
DO  - 10.1016/S0031-3955(16)34292-4
AB  - Although outcome for the infant between 1000 and 1500 gm immediately cared for in a perinatal center is good, the same cannot yet be said for infants under 1000 gm. The survival in this weight category continues to slowly improve; however, the morbidity seems to have plateaued since about 1975. Intracranial hemorrhage (periventricular-intraventricular) continues to be a common complication in infants born ≤32 weeks gestation. Over 40 per cent of infants <1500 gm routinely screened by ultrasound have periventricular-intraventricular hemorrhage and survivors of these lesions are at great risk for neurologic sequelae. Recent early follow-up studies indicate that infants with Grade III and IV intraventricular bleeds have a very high incidence of neurologic impairment, whereas those with lesser Grade I and II bleeds have no increased risk. It is hopeful that future perinatal research studies utilizing contemporary diagnostic techniques (intrapartum monitoring, CT scan, ultrasonography, electroencephalogram, noninvasive intracranial pressure monitoring) will provide perinatologists with additional information about the pathogenesis of bleeds in hypoxic prematures which will aid in preventing and/or ameliorating the most serious neurodevelopmental sequelae. The etiology of learning abnormalities in high-risk infants without major sequelae as yet remains unclear. A variety of clinical variables could possibly be playing a role including mild conductive hearing loss, visual impairments, and low socioeconomic status. The possibility of an underlying cerebral abnormality, however, exists. A recent paper by Takashima, et al. identified at autopsy a persistence of immature neurons in low-birth-weight infants suggestive of a neonatal delay or arrest. It may be that in the tiniest infants there are internal limits determinig outcome that even the most sophisticated perinatal care cannot overcome. Finally, we must continuously be alert to the possibility of introducing new iatrogenic hazards into the intensive care nursery. Clarification of these issues will occur only through continued perinatal collaboration in conjunction with longitudinal follow-up studies of high-risk infants. Analysis of perinatal variables relative to subsequent environmental and socioeconomic factors is imperative. The overall objective must be to provide quality medical care to the pregnant mother and her infant through the coordinated efforts of obstetricians, perinatologists, neonatologists, primary health care givers, and educators to ensure an end result of quality outcome.
