TY  - JOUR
TI  - Late preterem infants
AU  - Stanojević, M.
AU  - Rodin, U.
AU  - Bošnjak, A.P.
AU  - Erceg, M.
AU  - Hafner, T.
AU  - Nad, K.B.
PY  - 2012
JO  - Gynaecologia et Perinatologia
VL  - 21
IS  - SUUPL.1
SP  - 13
EP  - 22
DO  - Article
AB  - Prematurity rate in Croatia is for many years between 5.1% to 6.1%. The majority of preterm infants belong to the group born between 32 and 36 weeks of gestation, while infants below 31 to 22 weeks of gestation contribute with less than 1%. Preterm infants accounted in the years 2009 and 2010 for 75.1% of perinatal mortality, 73.3% of fetal mortality, and 77.8% of the early neonatal mortality rate in Croatia. Interestingly, premature infants between 32 and 36 weeks of gestation contribute with 30% in the fetal mortality rate and only with 14.8% in the early neonatal mortality rate. Until now much attention has been paid in Croatia to the care for very immature premature babies, while the health problems of late preterm infants (born between 34 and 36 weeks of gestation) in the papers of Croatian physicians were under-estimated. According to some research, late preterm infants require special attention because in the whole population of premature infants they contribute with 70% to 75%, and compared to term neonates they are more likely to become sick and to die in perinatal period and thereafter. It has been proved that late preterm infants had more health problems during both early and late neonatal period, with more feeding problems connected with poor weight gain and poor growth which can contribute to more probable readmission to the hospital after discharge from the maternity ward. Late preterm infants have more neurodevelopmental problems, cerebral palsy is more prevalent as well as attention deficit and hyperactivity disorders, learning difficulties, social adjustment disorders, and many others. The effect of health problems appearing in the late preterm infants on long-term neurodevelopmental outcome is largely unknown, but there is growing alarm that these infants are more vulnerable to brain injury and long-term neurologic sequels than previously appreciated. Furthermore, infants born during the late preterm period are at more than 3-fold increased risk of developing cerebral palsy compared with term infants. The last 6 weeks of gestation are a critical period of growth and development of the fetal brain. Brain weight at 34 weeks is only 65% of that of the term brain, and gyral and sulcal formation is incomplete. Cortical volume increases by 50% between 34 and 40 weeks' gestation, and 25% of cerebellar development occurs during the same period of time. Costs of health care and treatment of this group of preterm infants are much higher than the cost of health care for term neonates. Socioeconomic status of the mother as an independent factor significantly affects the respiratory and non-respiratory morbidity of late preterm infants. In this review the health problems of late preterm infants are presented with special accent on prevention of late preterm birth and some conditions like neurodevelopmental disorders which in late preterm infants may occur after birth, throughout the period of infancy, in the period of pre-school and school age, and later in life.
