AB  - A comparison concerning the alpha/theta + delta ratio was made between a group of 275 school-age children with encephalopathy risk factors at birth (low birthweight, dystocic labour, forceps and vacuum extraction, asphyxia, cesarean section for fetal distress) and a group of controls without risk factors, matched from the point of view of age, sex and socioeconomic status. Parametric and nonparametric tests were used. Statistically significant differences from the matched control group were found in the allha/theta + delta ratio of children with risk factors particularly in the prefrontotemporo-central region, especially in cases of low birthweight, dystocic labour and forceps extraction. Moderate asphyxia at birth, cesarean section and vacuum extraction determined insignificant quantitative abnormalities in the late EEG. Apart from the percentages of EEG changes, the percentages of occurrence of postnatal neuropsychiatric manifestations (paresis, febrile convulsions, enuresis, pavor, speech disturbances, psychomotor growth disturbances, behavioral disorders) are given: 43.2% in cases with dystocic labour, 37.5% in forceps extraction, 28% in vacuum extraction, 24% in asphyxia at birth, 18% in low birthweight and 12.8% in cesarean section. Neuro-psychiatric manifestations were also present in 16.2% of the children without perinatal encephalopathy risk factors.
