TI  - Nutrition: its relationship to children's physical, mental, and emotional development
AU  - Martin, H.P.
PY  - 1973
JO  - American Journal of Clinical Nutrition
VL  - 26
IS  - 7
SP  - 766
EP  - 775
DO  - 10.1093/ajcn/26.7.766
AB  - Prenatal undernutrition has a serious effect on the growth and development of the central nervous system of the fetus. This may be secondary to maternal undernutrition or to impaired fetal nutrition, such as placental insufficiency, with normal maternal nutrition. It is uncertain if mild undernutrition in a previously well nourished mother would have serious effects on a child. Undernutrition early in infancy has permanent effects on the growth and development of the central nervous system and on CNS function for years afterward. This has been demonstrated in animals as well as in humans. Because there is a tremendous growth in the central nervous system during the first 6 to 12 months of life in the human, this is a critical period for permanent organic effects on the brain. There is little data that undernutrition after l year of age permanently affects the central nervous system in terms of decreased cell number, cell size, or DNA composition. Nutritional deficiencies after l yr of life do affect the ability of the child to learn, to attend, and to sustain interest in learning tasks. This is compounded by an increased rate of infections in the undernourished child. Such deficits in learning may be reversed by nutritional intervention. Children who are retarded or brain damaged prenatally or perinatally may not grow well. The basis for impaired growth may be a condition affecting brain function and may be treatable or remediable. Feeding difficulties, common in the physically handicapped child, may need intervention to increase intake and absorption. Poor growth, not due to an organic cause, may be associated with abuse, emotional deprivation, neglect, rejection of the child, or poverty. Nutritional problems not based on an organic etiology are signs of a more pervasive pattern of suboptimal parental care. The causes may be economical or social but most often, in the US, are associated with psychic or social dysfunction. Protein and calorie deprivation during the period of rapid brain growth causes central nervous system damage. In societies in which breast feeding is still accepted, serious undernutrition does not occur in early infancy. Insistence on early and regular evaluations of the infant could assure assistance for any child who is not growing well in infancy. High risk children, particularly small for gestation babies, should be kept under careful professional surveillance. Inasmuch as learning ability, perceptual abilities, and attention span have been related to nutritional deficits, we must provide adequate nutrition to children in our society. Nutrition, diet, and feeding affect and are affected by pathology or deviations in all arenas of life: biologic, social, and psychic.
